\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
%
% TRANSFORMATION TNoExtraposition
< m1: CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [mu1,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}[mu2],
             mu3
            ]
>

< m : CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [mu1,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}[mu2],
             mu3
            ]
>

<
   SUBRULE (* 1. no complrel/SENT *)
      < mu2: [ I2:mu5 ] >
      < mu2: [ I2:mu5 ] >

      MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I2: NOT EXIST (mu5, [complrel/SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[mu4]])
           AND
           NOT EXIST (mu5, [prepobjrel/PREPP{PREPPrec1}
                                      [head/PREP{PREPrec1},
                                       objrel/I21::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}
                                             [mu4]
                                      ]
                           ] )
           I21: SENTENCErec1 = { finiteness : finite
                                 senttype   : subordinateclause
                                 mood       : declarative
                                 conjkey    : KEY_thatCONJkey }
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >

   SUBRULE (* 2. complrel/SENT{inf} *)
      < mu2: [ I3:mu6 ] >
      < mu2: [ I3:mu6 ] >

      MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I3: EXIST (mu6, [complrel/I31::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[mu4]])
           I31: SENTENCErec1.finiteness = infinite
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >

   SUBRULE (* 3. complrel/SENT{0-that} *)
      < mu2: [ I4: mu7 ] >
      < mu2: [ I4: mu7 ] >

      MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I4: EXIST (mu7, [complrel/I41::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[mu4]])
           I41: SENTENCErec1 = { finiteness : finite
                                 mood       : declarative
                                 senttype   : subordinateclause
                                 conjkey    : 0 }
       >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >

>
&
\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TNoExtraposition
\item[* relevant examples:] \mbox{}\\
\begin{enumerate}
\item subrule 1: No embedded sentence present, or present under a predrel/XP:\\
I do [see him], I can [see him], I counted on $_{NP}$[seeing him], I do [be 
able [to see him]], (it) did [not be [easy [getting the equipment loaded]]]
yesterday\\
\item subrule 2: embedded infinite sentence:\\
Closed Gerund: I did [watch [the moon rising over the mountains]] yesterday\\
YesNoQSent: He did [hesitate [whether to go there]] yesterday\\
WhQSent: He did [ask himself [where to go]] yesterday\\
ForToSent: I did [prefer [for John to go]] yesterday\\
Open ToSent: I did [decide [x1 to go]] yesterday\\
Closed ToSent: I did [expect [he to go]] yesterday\\
Closed InfSent: I did [see [the man leave]] yesterday\\
Open InfSent from subject position: (it) did [suprise me [to see you there]]
yesterday\\
(other Open InfSents occur only with modals, and have already been pruned)
\item subrule 3: embedded 0-thatSent:\\
?I did [discover [his colleagues lied to him]] yesterday\\
(it) did [surprise me [he never told you]] yesterday\\
If no ADVs may occur following the embedded sentence, the subrule can specify 
that mu3 must be empty here.
\end{enumerate}
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) english
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class)TC\_SentOKRules
\item[* date of creation:] 19-FEB-1988 11:51:01  
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:]
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to let sentences which have nothing to extrapose get through 
this transformation class.\\
 Note that no extraposition from subjrel/SENTENCES 
that occur in CLOSEDXPPROPs is required 
here (e.g.\ for anyone to escape does [be impossible] $\rightarrow$ 
- does [be impossible] for anyone to escape), since this
kind of extraposition (still without {\em it\/}-insertion), has already been 
taken care of in the substitution rule for subject sentences of XPPROPs, 
RxppExtrapSubjSentSubst. \\
Also note that SENTENCEs which occur under a prepobjrel or byobjrel are not 
extraposed either: - was [shocked I by what they asked me to do] yesterday,
I did not [agree with what they just told me] yesterday. If these sentences are 
heavy, they may be placed clause-finally by a heavy PP-shift rule (so the prep 
is moved together with the sentence).
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
