\begin{verbatim}

% TRANSFORMATION TExtraposition1
< m1: CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [mu1,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                    [I3:mu3,
                     complrel/I2::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[mu4],
                     I5:mu5
                    ],
             mu6
            ]
>

< m : CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [mu1,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                    [I3:mu3,
                     I5:mu5
                    ],
             mu6,
             extraposrel/I4::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[mu4]
            ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I2: SENTENCErec1 =  { finiteness : finite
                             senttype   : subordinateclause } 
           AND
           SENTENCErec1 <> { mood       : declarative
                             PROsubject : false
                             conjkey    : 0 } 
       I3: NOT EXIST (mu3, [I31:rel1/..])
           I31: rel1 IN AUX_finalVPAdvRels OR
                rel1 = byobjrel
       I4: (SENTENCErec1 =  { finiteness : finite
                              senttype   : subordinateclause } 
            AND
            SENTENCErec1 <> { mood       : declarative
                              PROsubject : false
                              conjkey    : 0 } 
           )
           OR
           SENTENCErec1 = { finiteness : infinite
                            senttype   : subordinateclause
                            PROsubject : true
                            infsort    : toinf }
       I5: ALL (mu5, I51:rel2/..)
           I51: rel2 IN AUX_finalVPAdvRels OR
                rel2 = byobjrel
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TExtraposition1
\item[* relevant examples:] \mbox{}\\
\begin{enumerate}
\item For finite sentences:\\
ASIFSENT: He did [act] yesterday [as if he had gone mad]\\
WhQSENT: He did [ask himself] yesterday [where he would go]\\
YesNoQSENT: He did [ask himself] yesterday [whether he should agree]\\
ThatSENT: He did [tell me] yesterday [that I shouldn't have done that]\\
ThatSENT in passive: - was [told I by my mother] yesterday [that he had left]\\
SubjThatSENT, extrap: - did [surprise me] yesterday [that he had left]\\
ThatSENT, pattern2: I did [believe it] yesterday [that I had won the lottery]
\item For infinite sentences in analysis only:\\
YesNoQSent: He did [hesitate] yesterday [whether to go there]\\
WhQSent: He did [ask himself] yesterday [where to go for a holiday this year]\\
Open ToSent: I did [decide] yesterday [x1 to go to the States next year]\\
SubjOpenInfSent, extrap: - did [surprise me] yesterday [to see you there]\\
SubjOpenGerund, extrap: - was not [easy] yesterday [getting the equipment
loaded]\\
Usually, these sentences will be subject to extraposition only when they are 
heavy, or when an adverb clash would occur (He didn't tell me yesterday where 
to go tomorrow). Since no measure for heaviness can be formulated yet in 
Rosetta, and since adverb clashes can hardly be predicted, analysis of such 
extraposed sentences always is allowed, but generation never takes place 
(to prevent malconstructions like {\em I promised her yesterday to go\/};
however, this also implies that we will generate {\em I decided to go to the 
States next year yesterday\/}).
\end{enumerate}
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) english
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class)TC\_SentOKRules
\item[* date of creation:] 19-FEB-1988 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] TNoExtraposition
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
