\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TPredExtrapos1
\item[* relevant examples:] \mbox{}\\
\begin{enumerate}
\item For finite sentences:\\
WhQSENT: He did [be doubtful] yesterday [where he would go]\\
YesNoQSENT: He did [be doubtful] yesterday [whether he should agree]\\
ThatSENT: He did [be certain] yesterday [that I would pass the test]\\
\item For infinite sentences in analysis only:\\
YesNoQSent: He did [be doubtful] yesterday [whether to go there]\\
WhQSent: He did [be doubtful] yesterday [where to go for a holiday this year]\\
Open ToSent: I did [be able] yesterday [x1 to go to the market before noon]\\
Usually, these infinite sentences will be subject to extraposition only when they are 
heavy, or when an adverb clash would occur (He wasn't able yesterday to tell me
where to go tomorrow). Since no measure for heaviness can be formulated yet in 
Rosetta, and since adverb clashes can hardly be predicted, analysis of such 
extraposed sentences always is allowed, but generation never takes place 
(to prevent malconstructions like {\em I was able yesterday to swim\/};
however, this also implies that we will generate {\em I was determined to go 
to the States next year yesterday\/}).
\end{enumerate}
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) english
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class)TC\_SentOKRules
\item[* date of creation:] 05-APR-1989
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] TExtraposition1
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 15/08/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Limited working to clauses with copula `be': for other xpprops, extraposition 
is delayed till after the control rules (in TC\_PPorSoutofNPAdjP), because 
these props have their own control rules (e.g. to deal with sentences without 
subject, which must be bound within the xpprop: he seemed afraid to leave)
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to extrapose finite sentences from under a predrel/XP
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) Only complrel 
sentences are extraposed; for other relations no rules have been written yet.
Note that it is assumed that the original position for a complrel/SENTENCE is 
always right behind the head/XP (X = ADJP, PREPP, NP, ADVP). If this proves to 
be incorrect, the rule should be reformulated.\\
In ADJPPROP, there also are postmodrel 
sentences (he was strong as if he were a lion) and toinfmodrel 
sentences (it was nice (for EMPTY) to look at, it was enough nice to dream of,
he was too old to learn new tricks), and Franciska also mentions the relations 
`postadjrel, causadvrel, toinfcomplrel, resultrel' in her sentence-substitution 
rules. If necessary, extraposition rules for these categories can be written 
once they have been formulated in the ADJPPROPtoFORMULA subgrammar (including 
the conditions on the place where the sentence has to go to in analysis).\\
No rule has been formulated to extrapose a sentence from a PREPP occurring in 
the predrel/XP. In the ADJPPROP subgrammar, a few pattern rules exist for 
sentences under a prepp, but the relevant example of a PREPTHATSENT is missing
and does not seem to exist. Other sentences need not extrapose (he was 
uncertain about where to go next year yesterday), or extrapose the whole 
(heavy) prepp (he was uncertain yesterday about where to go next year).
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] see above under 
`examples': no generation of extraposed infinite sentences
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
Perhaps a restriction should be formulated (as some kind of a speed measure) to 
limit the working of this transformation to cases where there is a subject. In 
cases there is no subject (and no ergative or passive, and a head verb `be'),
the analysis will still try to put the extraposed 
sentence in a complrel, while it should remain extraposed until 
RXppExtrapSubjSentSubst puts it in the empty subject position. Alternatively, 
the present rule could check whether the head of the XP allows for a sentence 
complement (easy for ADJP, where the attribute adjpatternefs is filled in the 
pattern rules)
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

